  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 906 PM MST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nCALM AND DRY CONDITIONS EXPECTED TUESDAY AND WEDNESDAY \n\nSnow showers are coming to an end over the central and southern\nmountains. Skies will clear while fog begins to develop in \nmountain valleys. A fast moving ridge will develop in the wake \nof this system. Clear and dry conditions are expected for \nTuesday and Wednesday as this ridge moves over the region. \n\n\nANOTHER ROUND OF SNOW EXPECTED AFTER THANKSGIVING  \n\nAfter this ridge moves through, deterministic guidance is coming\ninto better agreement that flow starts to split by Thursday. A \nridge begins developing over SoCal which will bring a cutoff low\nto our south while another powerful system moves to our north \nand east. A continuation of dry conditions with above normal \ntemperatures is expected for Thanksgiving as both systems move \naway from our CWA. By Friday, the SoCal ridge breaks down and \nanother system approaches our area. Flow turns northwesterly \nand with it, there's an increased chance of snowfall over the \nPark Range on Friday into Saturday. Confidence is still low on \nhow much snow will fall as some details remain uncertain. This \nis especially true if strong northwest flow sets up further \nnorth. If you are planning on traveling during this time, be \nprepared for the possibility of difficult travel conditions, \nespecially over the northern Colorado mountains. There is also a\npossibility of impacts south of the Park Range but confidence \nremains low on the exact details. After Friday and Saturday, \nguidance becomes more uncertain with multiple different \nscenarios. There is potential for the pattern to remain active \ninto Sunday and early next week.",
